consiSt("mn",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Minnesota Supreme Court","d":"Reviews Court of Appeals decisions only by discretionary petition for review (Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117) and may take a case early by accelerated review (Rule 118). Reviews the Tax Court and the Workers’ Compensation Court of Appeals directly by certiorari (Rule 116). Hears argued cases en banc (Rule 135). Controls bar admission, pro hac vice certification through the Board of Law Examiners, and lawyer discipline."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Minnesota Court of Appeals","d":"Hears appeals of right from district court judgments and the appealable orders listed in Rule 103.03, discretionary appeals under Rule 105, certiorari review of agency decisions, and declaratory challenges to administrative rules (Rule 114). Sits in panels of at least three judges. Issues precedential, nonprecedential, or order opinions; nonprecedential opinions are persuasive only (Rule 136.01). No petitions for rehearing (Rule 140.01)."},{"l":"TRIAL","n":"District Court","d":"The general trial court. Civil practice is governed by the Rules of Civil Procedure plus the statewide General Rules of Practice (Gen. R. Prac. 101–146 for civil cases), which also set motion deadlines, scheduling, ADR, and e-filing. Districts may have local rules only as approved by the Supreme Court (Minn. R. Civ. P. 83). Attorneys must e-file and e-serve statewide (Gen. R. Prac. 14.01(b)(1))."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Tax Court and Workers’ Compensation Court of Appeals","d":"Their decisions go straight to the Supreme Court by writ of certiorari (Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 116), and nonappealable orders may be reviewed by discretionary petition (Rule 105.01). Conciliation court actions and appeals where no jury is demanded, and other listed special proceedings, are carved out of the general civil scheduling rule (Gen. R. Prac. 111.01)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA), Minn. Stat. §§ 554.07–554.20","d":"Minnesota enacted UPEPA in 2024 (2024 Minn. Laws ch. 123, art. 18) and repealed the old anti-SLAPP law, Minn. Stat. §§ 554.01–554.06. The act took effect the day after final enactment (the codified savings clause puts that date at May 25, 2024) and applies to civil actions pending on or filed after that date, but it does not affect a cause of action asserted before May 25, 2024, or a motion under the old law about that claim (§ 554.19).\nWhy the change: the Revisor’s note to former § 554.02 records that the old statute was held unconstitutional as applied to claims at law alleging torts in Leiendecker v. Asian Women United of Minnesota, 895 N.W.2d 623 (Minn. 2017). The old law made the plaintiff prove by clear and convincing evidence that the defendant’s acts were not immune.","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.07, 554.19; 2024 Minn. Laws ch. 123, art. 18, §§ 16–17; Minn. Stat. § 554.02 (2022) & Revisor’s note","v":true},{"t":"Scope and the long list of exclusions","d":"Covers a cause of action based on the defendant’s (1) communication in a governmental proceeding, (2) communication on an issue under review in a governmental proceeding, or (3) exercise of speech, press, assembly, petition, or association rights under the U.S. or Minnesota Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nMinnesota added exclusions beyond the uniform act: claims against government units; certain public-health enforcement; commercial speech by sellers about their goods or services; crime-victim suits; real-property possession and title suits; bodily injury and wrongful death (unless reputation damage is claimed); insurance-code and insurance-contract claims; common-law fraud; family-law chapters 517–519A and protection, harassment, and vulnerable-adult orders; employment claims under chapters 175, 177, 178, 179, 179A, wrongful discharge, whistleblowing, and negligent supervision or retention (unless reputation damage is claimed); consumer-protection claims under chapters 325F and 325G; and any claim under federal law.\nThe commercial-speech, fraud, and consumer-protection exclusions do not apply to news-gathering and creative works, or to consumer reviews and ratings (§ 554.08(d)). The moving party must show the act applies; the responding party must show an exclusion applies (§ 554.13(a)).","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.08(b)–(d), 554.13(a)(1)–(2)","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60 days to move, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling clocks","d":"File the special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after being served with the pleading that asserts the claim, or later for good cause (§ 554.09).\nFiling stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery, until the ruling and the time to appeal it run out; the court may stay matters involving other parties on motion. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is necessary and not otherwise available (§ 554.10). Fee motions, voluntary dismissals, severance, unrelated motions, and public-safety injunctions are not stayed.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (extendable for allowed discovery or good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing (§§ 554.11, 554.14). The court considers the pleadings and summary-judgment-type evidence under Minn. R. Civ. P. 56.03 (§ 554.12).\nDismissal with prejudice follows if the responding party cannot make a prima facie case on each element, or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact (§ 554.13(a)(3)). Filing the motion waives no other defense (§ 554.20).","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.09–554.14, 554.20","v":true},{"t":"Fees are mandatory for a prevailing movant","d":"The court shall award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant. A prevailing responding party gets fees only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay.\nA plaintiff cannot dodge fees by dismissing: a voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the movant’s right to a ruling and fees, and a dismissal with prejudice counts as the movant prevailing (§ 554.13(b)–(c)).","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.13(b)–(c), 554.16","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: 30 days, as of right, and the whole case stops","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. The appeal must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order, half the usual 60-day civil appeal period under Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01. While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties are stayed (§ 554.10(c)). The statute grants the special appeal of right only to the moving party; a responding party whose claim is dismissed must use the ordinary appeal rules.\nThe act must be broadly construed to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association rights (§ 554.17) and construed for uniformity with other enacting states (§ 554.18).","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.10(c), 554.15, 554.17, 554.18; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice in district court: Board certification first, then a motion by Minnesota counsel","d":"A lawyer admitted in another jurisdiction who is retained for a particular case may appear pro hac vice in the court’s discretion on written application if: (1) the Minnesota Board of Law Examiners certifies the lawyer’s good standing and that the lawyer is not suspended or disbarred anywhere for discipline or disability in lieu of discipline; (2) the lawyer pays a non-refundable $450 fee to the Board; (3) a Minnesota-admitted lawyer also signs the pleadings and documents; and (4) that Minnesota lawyer accepts service and is present (or participates remotely in a remote hearing). After the first appearance the court may let the out-of-state lawyer proceed without Minnesota counsel present.\nAdmission is required to argue or to sign anything. In an urgent matter the court may let the lawyer proceed on a representation that an application will be promptly submitted.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.01(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Application and motion papers","d":"Apply electronically to the Board of Law Examiners with the fee and a certificate of good standing from the licensing authority where admitted (Rule 5.03).\nThen an active Minnesota lawyer who is attorney of record moves the admission, served on all parties, with (1) the applicant’s affidavit or declaration listing every Minnesota pro hac vice application in the past two years (caption, venue, file number, outcome) and (2) the Board application plus the Board’s good-standing confirmation. Pay any motion fee. The court is to exercise discretion ‘liberally’ to grant. Admission may be revoked for rule violations or sanctionable conduct. If local counsel drops out, another Minnesota lawyer must promptly substitute and file a notice of appearance (Rule 5.04).","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.03, 5.04","v":true},{"t":"Fee waivers are narrow","d":"The $450 fee is waived only for (1) pro bono representation of a person of limited means where the lawyer will not charge or seek fees, and (2) lawyers representing a federal, state, or local government entity. No other waivers may be requested or granted, including for related cases sharing common questions.\nPro hac vice is not needed where another rule exempts it, for example a foreign-case deposition subpoena under Minn. R. Civ. P. 45.06(b), and tribal attorneys in juvenile protection and adoption cases.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.02(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Pro hac vice lawyers must register for e-filing and are subject to Minnesota discipline","d":"The out-of-state lawyer is bound by all rules for Minnesota lawyers, including e-filing and the Registered User requirements of Gen. R. Prac. 14.02(a); if e-service on that lawyer is unavailable, service on Minnesota co-counsel is service on the party. Applying or appearing submits the lawyer to the Minnesota Rules of Professional Conduct, the Rules on Lawyers Professional Responsibility, and Minnesota court jurisdiction.\nE-filing is mandatory statewide for attorneys (Select Users) since July 1, 2016. Only Registered Users may e-file, and each must keep a monitored designated e-mail address for the life of the case and until appeal periods expire.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.05, 14.01(b)(1), 14.02(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate courts: separate pro hac vice motion","d":"No attorney may sign appellate pleadings or argue in the Court of Appeals or Supreme Court unless licensed in Minnesota or admitted pro hac vice by that court. A Minnesota-licensed lawyer moves the admission, with the applicant’s affidavit of good standing in another state or territory. The appellate rule names no fee. An amicus brief may be filed only by an attorney admitted under this rule (Rule 129.01(a), amended April 1, 2025).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 143.05, subd. 1; 143.06; 129.01(a)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (MRPC 5.5) and house counsel","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere and not disbarred or suspended may provide temporary services in Minnesota in association with an actively participating Minnesota lawyer; in or related to a pending or potential proceeding where the lawyer is or expects to be authorized; in ADR related to the home practice; or in matters for family members or reasonably related to the home practice.\nMinnesota-specific: an out-of-state lawyer may provide services that exclusively involve federal law, tribal law, or the law of a jurisdiction where the lawyer is licensed, if the client is told the lawyer is not licensed in Minnesota (Rule 5.5(d)). In-house lawyers practicing in Minnesota need a house counsel license (Admission Rules 9 and 10).","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b)–(d); Minn. R. Admission 9A, 10A","v":true},{"t":"Full admission without exam: 36 of 60 months","d":"Admission without examination requires active licensed practice in good standing for at least 36 of the 60 months before applying, with at least 1,000 hours a year in listed practice roles. Alternatively, transfer a scaled MBE score of 145+ (from a passed exam, then admitted) or a Legacy UBE score of 260+, within 36 months of the exam. The NextGen UBE transfer score has not yet been set. Fee: $1,250 (Rule 12F).\nLimited-scope appearances: no district-court rule on limited-scope appearances was found in the collected General Rules. MRPC 1.2(c) allows limited-scope representation with informed consent, and a lawyer who has appeared withdraws only by serving and filing a notice under Gen. R. Prac. 105, which does not entitle the client to a continuance.","c":"Minn. R. Admission 7A–7C, 12F; Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 1.2(c); Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 105","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Minnesota Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"Effective September 1, 1985, amended through July 1, 2026 in the Revisor’s compilation. The text tracks the ABA Model Rules numbering but has real substantive departures, listed below. Choice of law follows ABA Rule 8.5(b). A lawyer providing or offering legal services in Minnesota is subject to Minnesota discipline even if not admitted here (Rule 8.5(a)).","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 8.5","v":true},{"t":"Fees: no ‘nonrefundable’ fees; advance fees go in trust unless a signed flat-fee agreement says otherwise","d":"Fee payments received before services are rendered are presumed unearned and must be held in trust. A flat fee may be treated as the lawyer’s property on payment only under a written agreement signed by the client that discloses five listed items, including that the fee will not be held in trust and that the client may be entitled to a refund. Availability (true retainer) fees need a signed writing. Fee agreements may not call any fee ‘nonrefundable’ or ‘earned upon receipt.’ Unearned portions must be refunded on termination.\nThe ABA rule has no comparable text. Refusing to honor a final and binding fee arbitration award after agreeing to arbitrate is misconduct (Rule 8.4(i)).","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 1.5(b)(1)–(3), 8.4(i)","v":true},{"t":"Confidentiality: broader permissive disclosure","d":"Rule 1.6(b) lets a lawyer reveal client information in 11 situations. Unlike the ABA rule, these include: (2) non-privileged information the client has not asked to keep inviolate, if disclosure would not embarrass or likely harm the client; (4) to prevent a fraud using the lawyer’s services that will cause substantial financial injury, or to prevent the commission of a crime; (5) to rectify the consequences of a client’s crime or fraud that used the lawyer’s services; and (10) to report another lawyer’s serious misconduct to the Office of Lawyers Professional Responsibility.","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 1.6(b)","v":true},{"t":"Conflicts: narrower lateral screening; loan guarantees; sexual relations","d":"Lateral screening under Rule 1.10(b) cures a former-firm conflict only if confidential information the lawyer received is unlikely to be significant in the new matter, the lawyer is screened, and affected clients get timely notice. The ABA rule allows screening without the ‘unlikely to be significant’ condition.\nRule 1.8(e)(3) lets a lawyer guarantee a loan a client reasonably needs to withstand litigation delay, if the client stays liable and no promise was made before retention. Rule 1.8(j) adds Minnesota definitions and procedure for the sexual-relations ban, including an exception for firm clients the lawyer does not work on.","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 1.8(e)(3), 1.8(j), 1.10(b)","v":true},{"t":"Advocacy rules that differ: ex parte writings, trial publicity, witness requests","d":"Rule 3.5(g) allows a written communication on the merits to the judge if a copy is promptly delivered to opposing counsel, or an oral one on adequate notice. Rule 3.6 limits trial-publicity restrictions to extrajudicial statements likely to materially prejudice a jury trial in a pending criminal matter. It does not reach civil cases. Rule 3.4(f) (asking non-clients not to talk to the other side) follows the ABA text.","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 3.4(f), 3.5(g), 3.6","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: harassment and discrimination, and fee-arbitration awards","d":"Rule 8.4(g) bars harassment based on listed characteristics, including status with regard to public assistance and marital status, in connection with professional activities. Rule 8.4(h) bars discriminatory acts prohibited by federal, state, or local statute or ordinance that reflect adversely on fitness, judged by four listed factors. These differ from ABA Model Rule 8.4(g).\nRule 7.2(c) restricts claims of specialist certification unless the certifying body is named and, if it is not accredited by the Minnesota Board of Legal Certification, the communication says so.","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 8.4(g)–(i), 7.2(c)","v":true}],"style":"State of Minnesota","home":"https://mncourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Hip-pocket service: the action starts at service, and you must file within one year or lose it with prejudice","d":"A Minnesota civil action is commenced by service of the summons (or a signed waiver, or delivery to the sheriff followed by service within 60 days), not by filing. A case that is not filed with the court within one year after commencement against any party is deemed dismissed with prejudice against all parties, unless all parties signed a stipulation within that year to extend. Family cases are excepted. Calendar the one-year date the day you serve.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 3.01, 5.04(a)"},{"t":"Do not plead a dollar figure over $50,000","d":"If unliquidated damages over $50,000 are sought, the complaint must state only that reasonable damages greater than $50,000 are sought. Amounts under $50,000 must be stated.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 8.01"},{"t":"Appeals from orders run from service of notice of filing, not entry","d":"Appeal a judgment within 60 days after its entry. Appeal an appealable order within 60 days after any party serves written notice of its filing; the court administrator’s own notice does not start the clock (Minn. R. Civ. P. 77.04). If you won, serve notice of filing to start the appeal period. Once the time to appeal the judgment expires, earlier orders can no longer be appealed (Rule 104.02).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1; 104.02; Minn. R. Civ. P. 77.04"},{"t":"A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if filed within new-trial time, and a premature notice of appeal is void","d":"Timely motions under Rules 50.02, 52.02, 59, and (if filed within the new-trial deadline) 60 restart the appeal period from service of notice of filing of the order deciding the last motion. A notice of appeal filed while such a motion is pending is ‘premature and of no effect’; you must file a new one. A new-trial motion must be served within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the decision, and heard within 60 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subds. 2–3; Minn. R. Civ. P. 59.03"},{"t":"Use the notice to remove early or lose it","d":"The peremptory notice to remove must be served and filed within 10 days after notice of the assigned judge. It cannot be used against a judge who has already ruled on a motion or presided at another proceeding you had notice of.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.03"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP: 60 days to move, 30 days to appeal","d":"The UPEPA motion is due within 60 days after service of the pleading (later only for good cause), and an appeal from denial must be filed within 30 days after entry of the order, not the general 60 days. Fees to a prevailing movant are mandatory, and a plaintiff’s voluntary dismissal does not avoid them.","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.09, 554.13(b)–(c), 554.15, 554.16"},{"t":"No discovery until the Rule 26.06 conference and plan","d":"Parties may not seek discovery from any source before conferring and preparing a discovery plan, except in exempt proceedings or by stipulation or order. The conference must happen within 30 days from the initial answer due date, and the written plan must be filed within 14 days after the conference. Early Rule 34 requests may be delivered 21 days after service but are deemed served at the conference. Initial disclosures are due within 60 days after the original answer due date.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 26.01(a)(3), 26.04(a)–(b), 26.06(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Dispositive motions: 28 days’ notice, and summary judgment cutoff","d":"Dispositive motion papers (notice, motion, proposed order, affidavits, memorandum) must be served and filed at least 28 days before the hearing, or the hearing may be canceled. Unless the court orders otherwise, no summary judgment motion may be filed more than 30 days after the close of all discovery. Summary judgment memoranda need a record-cited statement of undisputed facts.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.03, 115.06; Minn. R. Civ. P. 56.02"},{"t":"Pro hac vice is a two-step process with a fee and local counsel at the first appearance","d":"Out-of-state lawyers cannot sign or argue until admitted. First get Board of Law Examiners certification and pay the $450 non-refundable fee; then Minnesota counsel moves admission with a two-year history of Minnesota pro hac vice applications. Minnesota counsel must co-sign everything and attend at least the first appearance. Appellate courts need their own pro hac vice motion.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 5.01–5.04; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 143.05"},{"t":"Restricted identifiers in a filing can get it rejected or struck","d":"Social security, taxpayer ID, and financial account numbers (other than the last four digits of an account) may not appear in filed documents; use Confidential Information Form 11.1 or a properly designated non-public filing. Non-compliant filings may be rejected; if one slips through, the administrator restricts it and you have 3 days to fix it or it is struck.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 11.02, 11.05; Minn. R. Civ. P. 5.04(c)(4)"},{"t":"No rehearing in the Court of Appeals; petition for review in 30 days","d":"The Court of Appeals does not entertain rehearing petitions. Seek Supreme Court review by a petition filed within 30 days of the filing of the decision, limited to 2,000 words, with a $550 fee. The appellate courts may not extend this deadline. Rehearing exists only in the Supreme Court (14 days), and not from denial of review.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subds. 1, 3; 126.02; 140.01"},{"t":"Motions to reconsider are prohibited without permission","d":"A district-court motion to reconsider may be brought only with the court’s express permission, granted on compelling circumstances. Ask by letter of no more than two pages, served on all parties. Such a request is not one of the post-decision motions that toll the appeal period under Rule 104.01, subd. 2.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.11; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 2"}],"judge":[{"t":"Notice to remove: one peremptory removal as of right, within 10 days","d":"Any party or attorney may serve and file a notice to remove the assigned judge or judicial officer within 10 days after receiving notice of who will preside at the trial or hearing, and no later than the start of the trial or hearing. No cause is needed.\nThe right is lost against a judge who has already presided at a motion or other proceeding of which the party had notice, and against a judge assigned by the Chief Justice. Those judges can be removed only by an affirmative showing of disqualification under the Code of Judicial Conduct. After one removal as of right, the substitute can be removed only for cause. The chief judge of the district reassigns the case.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.03","v":true},{"t":"Removal for actual prejudice or bias","d":"No judge may sit if disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct (Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.02). A motion to remove for actual prejudice or bias is heard first by the challenged judge. If denied, a motion to the chief judge (or a designee) for reconsideration must be filed and served within 7 days of the order.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 63.02; Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 106","v":true},{"t":"Referees and court-selected neutrals","d":"Object to having a referee hear a contested matter within 14 days of notice of the assignment, and before any hearing begins (Gen. R. Prac. 107). A party may remove a court-appointed ADR neutral, without consent, by notice within 7 days of the appointment; a substitute neutral can be removed only on a showing of prejudice (Gen. R. Prac. 114.04(c)).","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 107, 114.04(c)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate recusal motions: short fuses and panel review","d":"Supreme Court: move within 14 days after the case-initiating filing or the order granting review; the justice decides within 3 days after the response is due, and a denial can be reviewed within 5 days by a randomly drawn three-member panel of acting justices. Court of Appeals: move within 7 days after the notice of oral argument or nonoral conference (or of a panel change); review of a denial within 5 days goes to the chief judge and two random judges. Review motions are limited to 2,000 words.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 141.01–141.03","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"UPEPA must be read broadly, with media and consumer-review carve-backs","d":"Minnesota’s anti-SLAPP act must be broadly construed to protect speech, press, assembly, petition, and association. Its commercial-speech, fraud, and consumer-protection exclusions do not apply to news-gathering, journalism, or creative and audiovisual works, or to consumer opinions, reviews, and ratings of businesses.","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.08(a)(1), (d), 554.17","v":true},{"t":"Lawyer speech: publicity rule reaches only criminal jury matters","d":"Minnesota’s trial-publicity rule restricts only extrajudicial statements by lawyers in criminal matters that would likely prejudice a jury trial. Civil litigators are not covered by Rule 3.6, though other rules (for example 3.5, 4.4, 8.4(d)) still apply.","c":"Minn. R. Prof. Conduct 3.6","v":true},{"t":"No recording in courtrooms; remote hearings may not be recorded","d":"No visual or audio recording (including livestreaming) is allowed in courtrooms or courthouse areas during trials and hearings except as the rule permits, and a remote video hearing may not be recorded or broadcast unless the presiding judge specifically authorizes it.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 4.01","v":true}],"other":[{"t":"Statewide motion practice rules replace most local rules","d":"Dispositive motions: serve and file 28 days before the hearing; response 14 days; reply 7 days. Nondispositive: 21/14/7. Memoranda are capped at 35 pages, including any reply. The parties must meet and confer before any motion is heard. Motions to reconsider are prohibited without permission, sought by a letter of no more than 2 pages.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.03–115.05, 115.10, 115.11","v":true},{"t":"Personal leave continuance is automatic","d":"A timely application by an attorney substantially involved in the case for up to 90 days of leave (own health condition, birth or adoption, caring for a seriously ill family member, or a death in the family) is granted automatically, with client informed consent. An objection must be brought by motion within 14 days. Discovery is suspended during the leave unless ordered otherwise. A parallel appellate rule exists, but it never extends the time to file a notice of appeal.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 18; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 126.03","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Minnesota Rules of Civil Appellate Procedure (Revisor of Statutes compilation, amendments effective through April 1, 2025); Special Rules of Practice for the Minnesota Court of Appeals (through July 12, 2024); Minnesota Rules of Civil Procedure (through July 1, 2025) Rules 23.06, 50, 52, 54.02, 59, 60, 62, 77.04; Minn. Stat. § 554.15 (UPEPA). Official texts from revisor.mn.gov. Amendments effective after those dates could not be checked; the Judicial Branch rules-orders page refused the download script.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Minnesota: 60 days, but the trigger depends on whether you appeal a judgment or an order","d":"Civil appeals of right go to the Minnesota Court of Appeals; the Supreme Court reviews the Court of Appeals only by discretionary petition for review. The general appeal period is 60 days, running from entry of a judgment, but from service by any party of written notice of filing for an appealable order. Statutes can shorten it; for example, the UPEPA anti-SLAPP appeal is 30 days.\nThe appellate courts cannot extend the time to file a notice of appeal or to seek review. There is no rehearing in the Court of Appeals. Briefs are measured in words (14,000 principal, 7,000 reply), and an addendum replaces the old appendix.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.03, 104.01, 117, 126.02, 132.01, 140.01; Minn. Stat. § 554.15"},"groups":[["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"What to file, where, and the fee","d":"File a notice of appeal with the clerk of the appellate courts and serve it on adverse parties within the appeal period. The notice identifies the judgment or order and lists opposing counsel. File with it: proof of service, proof of filing a copy with the trial court administrator, a copy of the judgment or order, the statement of the case (Rule 133.03), and the $550 filing fee. Fee exemptions include in forma pauperis status, public defender or legal aid representation, and government appellants.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.01, subds. 1, 3; 133.03"},{"t":"Appealable judgments and orders","d":"Appeal lies from a final judgment or a Rule 54.02 partial judgment; orders granting, refusing, or dissolving injunctions; orders vacating or sustaining attachment; orders denying a new trial (or granting one, if expressly based only on errors of law); orders that in effect determine the action; final orders in supplementary proceedings; final orders in special proceedings affecting a substantial right; orders granting or denying modification of custody, visitation, maintenance, or child support; orders denying a Rule 12 failure-to-state motion or summary judgment if the trial court certifies the question as important and doubtful; and other orders made appealable by statute or case law.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.03"},{"t":"Time: 60 days, with different triggers","d":"Unless a statute provides otherwise: from a judgment, 60 days after its entry; from an appealable order, 60 days after service by any party of written notice of its filing. A Rule 54.02 partial judgment is appealable within 60 days only if the court expressly finds no just reason for delay and directs entry; otherwise time runs from the final judgment. The court administrator’s notice of orders does not limit the time to appeal (Minn. R. Civ. P. 77.04).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1; Minn. R. Civ. P. 54.02, 77.04"},{"t":"Earlier orders die with the judgment’s appeal period","d":"No order made before entry of judgment is appealable after the time to appeal the judgment expires. Inserting costs and disbursements into the judgment later does not extend the time. On appeal from a judgment, the court may review any order involving the merits or affecting the judgment.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.04, 104.02"},{"t":"No extensions of the appeal deadline","d":"The appellate court may extend most deadlines for good cause, but not the time to file a notice of appeal or to seek review, except as specifically authorized by law. A personal leave continuance does not extend it either. Subdivision 2 of Rule 103.01 allows relief only where a good-faith notice was filed and served but later steps were missed.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.01, subd. 2; 126.02; 126.03(b)"}]],["Tolling and post-decision motions",[{"t":"Which motions restart the clock","d":"A proper and timely motion for judgment as a matter of law (Rule 50.02), to amend or make findings or alter the judgment (Rule 52.02), for a new trial (Rule 59), or for Rule 60 relief filed within the time for a new-trial motion restarts the appeal period for all parties. The period then runs from service by any party of notice of filing of the order deciding the last such motion.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 2"},{"t":"Post-trial motion deadlines","d":"Serve a new-trial motion within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the decision or order, and have it heard within 60 days (extendable only within that period for good cause). Motions for JMOL and amended findings follow the same timing. A stay of entry of judgment does not extend these times. The court may grant a new trial on its own initiative within 14 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 50.02, 52.02, 58.02, 59.03, 59.05, 59.06"},{"t":"Premature notices of appeal are void","d":"A notice of appeal filed before a tolling motion is decided is premature and of no effect and does not divest the trial court of jurisdiction. File a new notice within the period measured from service of notice of filing of the order deciding the motion; no second filing fee is charged.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 3"},{"t":"Motions to reconsider do not toll","d":"District-court motions to reconsider are allowed only with the court’s permission, requested by letter of no more than 2 pages. They are not among the motions listed in Rule 104.01, subd. 2.","c":"Minn. Gen. R. Prac. 115.11; Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 2"}]],["Cross-appeals and related appeals",[{"t":"Notice of related appeal: 14 days, $100","d":"After a timely notice of appeal, any other party may seek review of a judgment or order in the same action by a notice of related appeal, served and filed within 14 days after service of the first notice or within the ordinary appeal period, whichever ends later. File it with a $100 fee, a statement of the case, and a copy of the order if different. No cost bond unless ordered. This replaced the old notice of review; a respondent who wants affirmative relief must use it.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.02, subd. 2; 104.01, subd. 4; 106"},{"t":"Cross-appeal briefing","d":"When adverse parties both appeal, the first to file is the appellant (the plaintiff below if same day). Four briefs: appellant’s principal brief; respondent/cross-appellant’s combined brief within 30 days (16,500 words); appellant/cross-respondent’s response and reply within 30 days (10,000 words); cross-appellant’s reply within 14 days (7,000 words).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 4"}]],["Record and transcripts",[{"t":"Order the transcript within 14 days","d":"Within 14 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must order the needed transcript, file a notice to proceed by statement of the proceedings or agreed statement, or notify the respondent that none will be ordered. If ordering only part, serve a description and statement of issues; the respondent then has 14 days to order more. A transcript certificate signed by counsel and the reporter is due within 14 days after ordering; estimated completion may not exceed 60 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 110.02, subds. 1–2"},{"t":"Statements of the proceedings","d":"If there is no transcript, the appellant files a proposed statement within 14 days after the notice of appeal; the respondent may object within 14 days; the trial court’s approval must be filed within 60 days of the notice of appeal. An agreed statement also requires trial court approval within 60 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 110.03, 110.04"}]],["Briefs",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s brief and addendum: 30 days after delivery of the transcript (or the trial court’s approval of a statement); if no transcript is needed or it was obtained before appeal, 30 days after the notice of appeal or the order granting review. Respondent’s brief: 30 days after service of the appellant’s brief (or of a respondent-ordered transcript, if later). Reply: 14 days after the respondent’s brief or an amicus brief. Extensions require a Rule 127 motion within the original time, with an affidavit showing diligence.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subds. 1–3; 131.02"},{"t":"Length limits","d":"Principal briefs: 45 pages, or 14,000 words, or 1,300 monospaced lines. Reply and amicus briefs: 20 pages, or 7,000 words, or 650 lines. Word-count briefs need a certificate naming the software. A motion to file an enlarged brief must be filed at least 14 days before the brief is due. Proportional fonts must be at least 13-point.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 132.01, subds. 1, 3"},{"t":"Required contents","d":"The appellant’s statement of issues must show, for each issue, how it was raised below with record cites, the ruling, how it was preserved, and up to four apposite cases. The argument must state the standard of review for each issue. A Court of Appeals brief may say whether the opinion should be precedential. The reply is limited to new matter.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 128.02, subds. 1–4"},{"t":"Addendum, not appendix","d":"Appendices are no longer permitted. The appellant must file a separate addendum with a table of contents and the orders, judgment, findings, and memoranda on appeal, limited to 50 additional pages. Cite the record by Document Index Number where available. Unpublished opinions are generally not included.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 128.03, 130.01, 130.02"},{"t":"Amicus curiae","d":"Request leave within 14 days after the notice of appeal or order granting review (or within 14 days after a petition for review is filed). The request is limited to 1,500 words. Only an attorney admitted in Minnesota or admitted pro hac vice by the appellate court may file an amicus brief.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 129.01"}]],["Stays, bonds, and motions",[{"t":"No automatic stay; move first in the trial court","d":"An appeal does not stay enforcement unless the trial court orders a stay under Rule 108.02. A stay, approval of security, or injunction pending appeal must be sought first in the trial court, and the trial court’s ruling may be reviewed by motion in the Court of Appeals. For money judgments, security normally covers the unpaid judgment, costs, interest during appeal, and delay damages. A timely appeal suspends the trial court’s authority over the order appealed (except in injunction appeals) but not over collateral matters.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 108.01, 108.02; Minn. R. Civ. P. 62.02, 62.03"},{"t":"No cost bond unless ordered","d":"No cost bond is required unless the trial court, on motion and a showing of extraordinary circumstances, orders one, capped at $1,000.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 107.01, 107.02"},{"t":"Motion practice","d":"Responses are due 5 days after service; replies 3 days later, when the motion is submitted. These periods exclude intermediate weekends and holidays. No oral argument unless ordered. Filing a motion does not stay any deadline.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 127"}]],["Argument, decision, costs, and rehearing",[{"t":"Oral argument","d":"Argument is allowed unless no party requested it in the statement of the case, a brief was untimely, it was waived, any party is unrepresented, or the court finds it unnecessary. Court of Appeals: appellant 15 minutes plus 5 for rebuttal, respondent 15. Supreme Court: appellant up to 35 minutes, respondent up to 25.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 134.01, 134.03"},{"t":"Opinions and judgment","d":"Court of Appeals opinions may be precedential, nonprecedential, or order opinions; the latter two are not binding but nonprecedential opinions may be cited as persuasive. Judgment is entered no sooner than 30 days after the decision; a petition for review or rehearing stays entry.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 136.01, 136.02"},{"t":"Costs and appellate fees","d":"Prevailing party costs: $300 on a merits judgment. File a notice of taxation of costs and disbursements within 14 days of the decision; objections within 7 days. Motions for appellate attorney fees must be made by Rule 127 motion within the time for taxation of costs; response within 14 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 139.01, 139.03, 139.05"},{"t":"Rehearing only in the Supreme Court","d":"No rehearing in the Court of Appeals. In the Supreme Court, petition within 14 days after the decision, with a $100 fee; the opponent may answer within 7 days. No rehearing of a denial of review or accelerated review. A petition stays entry of judgment.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 140.01–140.03"},{"t":"Default dismissal","d":"If the appellant’s brief is 30 days overdue and the respondent has not moved, the court dismisses the appeal without notice, subject to a motion to reinstate showing good cause, merit, and no substantial prejudice.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 142.02"}]],["Review in the Minnesota Supreme Court",[{"t":"Petition for review","d":"File within 30 days of the filing of the Court of Appeals decision, with proof of service and a $550 fee. The petition is limited to 2,000 words. Review is discretionary; listed criteria include an important question, a ruling on a statute’s constitutionality, departure from usual course, and the need to develop, clarify, or harmonize law with statewide impact or likely recurrence. Failing to take other steps does not require dismissal.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subds. 1–3"},{"t":"Response and conditional cross-review","d":"A response is due within 21 days of service. The respondent may conditionally ask for review of additional issues; the petitioner then may not respond unless the court asks. Amicus applicants who want to argue whether to grant review must apply within 14 days after the petition is filed.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subds. 4–5"},{"t":"Accelerated review","d":"Any party may petition for accelerated review of a case pending in the Court of Appeals if it is of imperative public importance. Fee $100, 4,000-word limit. Filing does not stay Court of Appeals deadlines.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 118"}]],["Interlocutory and special appeals",[{"t":"Discretionary review of nonappealable orders","d":"Petition the Court of Appeals within 30 days of the filing of the order (not orders made during trial), with a $550 fee and a copy filed in the trial court. The petition is limited to 4,000 words and must explain why an immediate appeal is necessary. Response 7 days, reply 3 days (2,000 words). If granted, file a statement of the case within 7 days.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105.01–105.03"},{"t":"Certified questions on denial of dismissal or summary judgment","d":"An order denying a failure-to-state motion or summary judgment is appealable as of right only if the trial court certifies the question as important and doubtful.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 103.03(i)"},{"t":"Class certification orders","d":"The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification; apply within the Rule 105 time. The appeal does not stay district court proceedings unless ordered.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 23.06"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials: 30 days, as of right","d":"A UPEPA movant may appeal as of right from an order denying the special motion in whole or part within 30 days after entry of the order. All proceedings between all parties are stayed during the appeal.","c":"Minn. Stat. §§ 554.10(c), 554.15"},{"t":"Extraordinary writs","d":"Mandamus or prohibition against a trial court is sought by petition to the Court of Appeals ($550 fee); respondents may answer within 7 days. In emergencies, an oral petition may be made through the Chief Staff Attorney (Court of Appeals) or the Supreme Court Commissioner. Denial of a writ by the Court of Appeals is reviewed by petition for review, not a new writ.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 120.01–120.05, 121"}]],["E-filing and time computation",[{"t":"Appellate e-filing","d":"Filing is by the appellate courts’ electronic system where required by Supreme Court order (that order was not collected). Documents e-filed by 11:59 p.m. are filed that day if accepted. Use searchable PDF, Word, or WordPerfect. Documents that must also go to the trial court (such as the notice of appeal) may be served through the district court system, but separate proof of service must then be filed in the appellate court. Fax filing is not allowed without leave.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 125.01, 125.04, 132.03"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Rule 6.01 of the Civil Rules governs. Add 3 days for service by U.S. mail only, and 1 day for electronic or personal service after 5:00 p.m. E-service is complete on confirmation from the appellate system.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 125.03, 126.01; Minn. R. Civ. P. 6.01"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal from a judgment","d":"60 days from entry of the judgment.\nUnless a statute sets a different time. Not extendable by the appellate court (Rule 126.02).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment)","note":"Unless a statute sets a different time. Not extendable by the appellate court (Rule 126.02)."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal from an appealable order","d":"60 days from service by any party of written notice of the order’s filing.\nThe court administrator’s notice does not start this period.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 1","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service by any party of written notice of the order’s filing)","note":"The court administrator’s notice does not start this period."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal after a tolling motion","d":"60 days from service by any party of notice of filing of the order deciding the last tolling motion.\nRule 60 motions toll only if filed within the new-trial deadline. Notices filed while the motion is pending are void.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subds. 2–3","due":{"n":60,"from":"the trigger date (service by any party of notice of filing of the order deciding the last tolling motion)","note":"Rule 60 motions toll only if filed within the new-trial deadline. Notices filed while the motion is pending are void."}},{"t":"Notice of related appeal (cross-appeal)","d":"14 days from service of the first notice of appeal (or the ordinary appeal period, if later).\n$100 fee and statement of the case.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 104.01, subd. 4","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the first notice of appeal)","note":"$100 fee and statement of the case."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial (service)","d":"30 days from general verdict, or service of notice of filing of the decision or order.\nMust be heard within 60 days. JMOL (50.02) and amended findings (52.02) use the same times.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. P. 59.03","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (general verdict, or service of notice of filing of the decision or order)","note":"Must be heard within 60 days. JMOL (50.02) and amended findings (52.02) use the same times."}},{"t":"Petition for discretionary review (Court of Appeals)","d":"30 days from filing of the order.\nResponse 7 days; reply 3 days. Also the deadline for class-certification appeals (Minn. R. Civ. P. 23.06).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105.01","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the order)","note":"Response 7 days; reply 3 days. Also the deadline for class-certification appeals (Minn. R. Civ. P. 23.06)."}},{"t":"Order transcript / file notice re record","d":"14 days from filing the notice of appeal.\nTranscript certificate due 14 days after ordering.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 110.02, subd. 1","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing the notice of appeal)","note":"Transcript certificate due 14 days after ordering."}},{"t":"Appellant’s brief and addendum","d":"30 days from delivery of the transcript (or approval of a statement); if no transcript, filing of the notice of appeal.\n14,000 words.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 1","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (delivery of the transcript)","note":"14,000 words."}},{"t":"Respondent’s brief","d":"30 days from service of the appellant’s brief.\nOr 30 days after delivery of a respondent-ordered transcript, if later.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 2","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the appellant’s brief)","note":"Or 30 days after delivery of a respondent-ordered transcript, if later."}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the respondent’s (or amicus) brief.\n7,000 words; new matter only.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 131.01, subd. 3","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the respondent’s)","note":"7,000 words; new matter only."}},{"t":"Motion to file enlarged brief","d":"14 days from before the brief’s due date.\nMust be filed at least 14 days before the brief is due.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 132.01, subd. 3","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Must be filed at least 14 days before the brief is due."}},{"t":"Response to appellate motion","d":"5 days from service of the motion.\nReply 3 days. Weekends and holidays excluded.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 127","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply 3 days. Weekends and holidays excluded."}},{"t":"Petition for review (Supreme Court)","d":"30 days from filing of the Court of Appeals decision.\n$550 fee; 2,000 words. Response within 21 days of service.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 117, subd. 1","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Court of Appeals decision)","note":"$550 fee; 2,000 words. Response within 21 days of service."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing (Supreme Court only)","d":"14 days from filing of the Supreme Court decision or order.\nNone in the Court of Appeals. $100 fee.","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 140.01","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the Supreme Court decision or order)","note":"None in the Court of Appeals. $100 fee."}},{"t":"Notice of taxation of costs and disbursements","d":"14 days from filing of the appellate decision.\nObjections within 7 days. Fee motions due within the same time (Rule 139.05).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 139.03","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the appellate decision)","note":"Objections within 7 days. Fee motions due within the same time (Rule 139.05)."}},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP (UPEPA) appeal from denial","d":"30 days from entry of the order denying the special motion.\nAppeal of right for the moving party only; stays the whole action.","c":"Minn. Stat. § 554.15","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying the special motion)","note":"Appeal of right for the moving party only; stays the whole action."}},{"t":"Recusal motion, Court of Appeals","d":"7 days from notice of oral argument or nonoral conference (or panel change).\nSupreme Court: 14 days after the case-initiating filing or grant of review (Rule 141.01(b)).","c":"Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 141.02(b)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (notice of oral argument or nonoral conference)","note":"Supreme Court: 14 days after the case-initiating filing or grant of review (Rule 141.01(b))."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://mncourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Minnesota Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules; proportionality","s":"Govern civil suits in the district courts, except as stated in Rule 81, and are to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. Adds an express duty of the court and parties to keep process and costs proportionate to the amount in controversy and the complexity and importance of the issues.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the ‘civil action.’","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"3.01","t":"Commencement of the action","s":"An action is commenced against each defendant when the summons is served on that defendant, when the defendant signs a Rule 4.05 waiver, or when the summons is delivered to the sheriff of the defendant’s county (by mail, courier, or agreed electronic means) if service or first publication follows within 60 days. Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 3, filing does not commence the action; filing within one year is required by Rule 5.04(a).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"3.02","t":"Service of complaint","s":"A copy of the complaint must be served with the summons, except for service by publication under Rule 4.04.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.01","t":"Summons; form","s":"The summons states the court and parties, is signed by plaintiff or counsel, gives a U.S. address for service on the signer, states the time to answer, and warns of default. A Minnesota address is no longer required (2021).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.02","t":"By whom served","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, the sheriff or any nonparty at least 18 years old may serve a summons or other process.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.03","t":"Personal service within the state","s":"Individuals: personal delivery or abode service on a person of suitable age and discretion residing there; extra steps for persons in state institutions, judicial branch employees (via the court administrator or Clerk of Appellate Courts), and minors under 14 (also serve a parent or custodian). Partnerships and associations: a member or managing agent. Corporations: an officer, managing agent, or authorized or statutory agent. The State: the attorney general or a deputy or assistant. Counties, cities, towns, school districts, and other public bodies: the listed officers.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.04","t":"Service by publication; out-of-state and foreign service","s":"Publication confers jurisdiction only in listed cases (absconding residents, quasi in rem after attachment or garnishment, dissolution when ordered, property in Minnesota, mortgage or lien foreclosure). It requires filing the complaint and an affidavit, three weeks’ published notice, and is complete 21 days after first publication. Personal service outside the state has the same effect as publication. Service abroad may use Hague Convention or other agreed means, foreign law, letters rogatory, personal delivery, signed-receipt mail sent by the court administrator, or court-directed means.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.041","t":"Real property description in publication","s":"Publication in actions affecting real property must describe the property and state the object of the action.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.042","t":"Complaint after publication","s":"If a defendant served by publication appears within 14 days after service is complete, the plaintiff must serve the complaint within 7 days, and the defendant then has at least 21 days to answer.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.043","t":"Defense after publication; restitution","s":"A defendant served by publication without actual notice may be allowed to defend before judgment, and (except in dissolution) within one year after judgment, on just terms, with restitution if the defense succeeds.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.044","t":"Nonresident landowner’s agent","s":"If a nonresident landowner appoints an agent under Minn. Stat. § 557.01, serve the agent or principal under Rule 4.03; publication against the principal is not allowed.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.05","t":"Waiving service of summons","s":"Plaintiff may mail a waiver request with the complaint and two copies of Form 22B, giving 30 days (60 if abroad) to return it. A U.S. defendant who refuses without good cause must pay later service costs and fees for the motion to collect them. A defendant who timely waives has 60 days from the request (90 if abroad) to answer. The action is treated as served on the date the waiver is signed. Waiver does not waive jurisdiction or venue objections. Modeled on Fed. R. Civ. P. 4(d).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.06","t":"Return; proof of service","s":"Proof is by the sheriff’s certificate, the server’s affidavit, the written admission of the party served, or for publication the printer’s affidavit, stating time, place, and manner. Failing to file proof does not invalidate service.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"4.07","t":"Amendment of process","s":"The court may allow process or proof of service to be amended unless substantial rights of the person served would be prejudiced.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.01","t":"Service of later papers; appearance","s":"Orders required to be served, later pleadings, written motions (other than ex parte), and notices, demands, offers of judgment, and similar documents must be served on every party. Parties in default for failure to appear need not be served except with new claims, which require Rule 4 service. A party appears when it serves or files any document.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.02","t":"How service is made; e-service","s":"Serve the attorney of a represented party unless the court orders otherwise. Where Gen. R. Prac. 14 requires or permits e-service, use the E-Filing System. Otherwise deliver (hand, office, or abode) or mail to the last known address. Mail service is complete on mailing; e-service through the court system is complete on transmission. Gen. R. Prac. 14.01(c) gives relief for technical failures.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.03","t":"Numerous defendants","s":"The court may order that defendants’ pleadings need not be served on one another, with cross-claims and affirmative matter deemed denied.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.04","t":"Filing; one-year deadline; certificate of service; rejection","s":"(a) An action not filed with the court within one year after commencement against any party is deemed dismissed with prejudice as to all parties, unless the parties sign a stipulation to extend within that year; family cases are excluded. (b) Later documents must be filed within a reasonable time after service with a certificate of service signed under oath or penalty of perjury; discovery requests, responses, disclosures, and depositions are not filed unless the court orders. (c) Filings may be rejected only for missing fee or file number, wrong court, unauthorized discovery filings, or restricted identifiers; (d) the court may relate a rejected filing back to its original tender. No federal counterpart to the one-year rule.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.05","t":"Filing by fax","s":"Where e-filing is not required, documents may be faxed to the court on approved equipment; filing is complete on receipt. Within 7 days, send a $25 transmission fee per 50 pages, bulky exhibits, and filing fees. Keep the original.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5.06","t":"Filing electronically","s":"Where required or authorized by Supreme Court order or Gen. R. Prac. 14, an e-filed document is deemed filed when transmitted to the E-Filing System, and is date-stamped if accepted by the administrator.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"5A","t":"Notice of constitutional challenge","s":"A party challenging a federal or state statute where the U.S. or the State is not a party must promptly file a notice of constitutional question and serve it with the challenging document by U.S. mail on the U.S. Attorney General or Minnesota Attorney General.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"6.01","t":"Computing time","s":"Exclude the trigger day, count all days, include the last day unless it is a weekend or legal holiday. Periods under 7 days exclude weekends and holidays only if another rule expressly says so. Hour periods count every hour. If the administrator’s office is inaccessible, time extends. For e-filing, the last day ends at 11:59 p.m. Minnesota time. Legal holidays include days the U.S. mail does not operate. Add 3 days after service by U.S. mail, and 1 day after service by other means made after 5:00 p.m.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"6.02","t":"Enlarging time","s":"The court may extend time for cause on request made before the period expires, or after it expires on motion showing excusable neglect. It may not extend time under Rules 4.043, 59.03, 59.05, or 60.02 except as those rules allow.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"6.03","t":"Expiration of term","s":"A term of court’s expiration does not affect time periods or the court’s power.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"6.04","t":"Motion deadlines","s":"Deadlines for serving and filing motions and supporting or opposing papers are set by the General Rules of Practice (see Gen. R. Prac. 115). Rule 6.05 is abrogated.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"7.01","t":"Pleadings allowed","s":"Complaint and answer; reply to a counterclaim denominated as such; answer to a cross-claim; third-party complaint and answer. The court may order a reply to an answer. Demurrers and pleas are abolished.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"7.02","t":"Motions and other documents","s":"Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Rule motions require notice and hearing unless the rule allows ex parte relief. Parties may agree to written submission; the court may hear motions by telephone. Form and Rule 11 signing rules apply.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.01","t":"Claims for relief; damages over $50,000","s":"A short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment; if money is sought the amount must be stated. For unliquidated damages under $50,000 state the amount; for more than $50,000, state only that reasonable damages greater than $50,000 are sought. The federal rule has no such cap on stating amounts.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.02","t":"Defenses; form of denials","s":"State defenses in short and plain terms and admit or deny averments; lack of knowledge has the effect of a denial; denials must fairly meet the substance; general denials only if all averments are controverted in good faith.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.03","t":"Affirmative defenses","s":"Must be pleaded affirmatively, including accord and satisfaction, arbitration and award, assumption of risk, contributory negligence, discharge in bankruptcy, duress, estoppel, failure of consideration, fraud, illegality, injury by fellow servant, laches, license, payment, release, res judicata, statute of frauds, statute of limitations, and waiver. Misdesignated defenses or counterclaims are treated as properly designated.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.04","t":"Effect of failure to deny","s":"Averments other than the amount of damage are admitted if not denied where a response is required; otherwise deemed denied.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.05","t":"Concise pleading; alternative and inconsistent claims","s":"Averments must be simple, concise, and direct. Alternative, hypothetical, and inconsistent claims or defenses are allowed, subject to Rule 11.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"8.06","t":"Construction","s":"Pleadings are construed to do substantial justice.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity need not be pleaded; challenge it by specific negative averment (9.01). Fraud and mistake must be pleaded with particularity; state of mind generally (9.02). Conditions precedent may be pleaded generally but denied specifically (9.03). Official acts and ordinances by title and date (9.04); judgments without jurisdictional facts (9.05). Time and place are material (9.06). Special damages must be specifically stated (9.07). Unknown parties may be designated by any name and later substituted (9.08).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption with court, county, title, file number, Rule 7 designation, and, in the upper right corner, the case type from the State Court Administrator’s Case Type Index; name the assigned judge if one is assigned for all purposes (10.01). Pseudonyms or initials only if authorized by law or order. Numbered paragraphs and separate counts (10.02); adoption by reference and exhibits (10.03). A filing without the case type may be stricken unless promptly corrected (10.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"11.01","t":"Signature","s":"Every pleading and motion must be signed by an attorney of record or the self-represented party, with address, phone, e-mail, and attorney registration number. Unsigned documents are stricken unless promptly corrected. Filing through the E-Filing System certifies compliance with signature rules.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"11.02","t":"Representations to court","s":"Presenting a document certifies, after reasonable inquiry, that it has no improper purpose, legal contentions are warranted, factual contentions and denials have or will likely have support, and (Minnesota addition) the document contains no restricted identifiers and these have been submitted confidentially under Gen. R. Prac. 11. A motion about restricted identifiers need not wait the 21-day safe harbor.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"11.03","t":"Sanctions","s":"Sanctions motions must be separate and served but not filed unless the challenged matter is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days. The court may act on its own by show-cause order. Sanctions are limited to deterrence; no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal contentions, or on the court’s initiative after voluntary dismissal or settlement. Tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 11(c).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"11.04","t":"Discovery excluded","s":"Rule 11 does not apply to discovery requests, responses, objections, and motions under Rules 26–37.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.01","t":"When to answer","s":"Answer within 21 days after service of the summons (unless Rule 4.043 applies); answer a cross-claim and reply to a counterclaim within 21 days. If a Rule 12 motion is denied or postponed, respond within 14 days after notice of the court’s action; after a more definite statement, within 14 days.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.02","t":"Defenses by motion","s":"Lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, insufficient process or service, failure to state a claim, and failure to join a Rule 19 party may be raised by motion before pleading. A failure-to-state motion that relies on matters outside the pleadings becomes a Rule 56 motion. Minnesota’s list omits improper venue (the rules do not change venue; Rule 82).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.03","t":"Judgment on the pleadings","s":"Any party may move after pleadings close, without delaying trial; outside matters convert it to summary judgment.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.04","t":"Preliminary hearing","s":"Rule 12.02 and 12.03 defenses are decided before trial on application unless deferred.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.05","t":"More definite statement","s":"Available for vague pleadings or Rule 10.02 violations before responding; if the order is not obeyed within 14 days after notice, the court may strike the pleading.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.06","t":"Motion to strike","s":"Move before responding (or within 21 days after service if no response is permitted); the court may strike sham pleadings and insufficient, redundant, immaterial, impertinent, or scandalous matter.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"12.07–12.08","t":"Consolidation and waiver of defenses","s":"Omitting an available Rule 12 defense from a Rule 12 motion bars a later motion on it. Personal jurisdiction, process, and service defenses are waived if omitted from a Rule 12 motion or from the answer (or a Rule 15.01 amendment as of course). Failure to state a claim and failure to join an indispensable party survive to trial; lack of subject-matter jurisdiction is never waived.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaims and cross-claims","s":"Compulsory counterclaims arising out of the same transaction must be pleaded unless they require absent parties or were already pending (13.01). Permissive counterclaims are allowed (13.02) and may exceed the claim (13.03). The rules do not enlarge counterclaims against the State (13.04). Later-acquired and omitted counterclaims need leave (13.05, 13.06). Cross-claims against co-parties arising from the same transaction (13.07). Joinder of additional parties under Rules 19–20 (13.08). Separate judgment under Rules 42.02 and 54.02 (13.09).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may serve a third-party summons and complaint on one who may be liable to it, but needs consent of all parties or leave of court if it files the third-party complaint more than 90 days after it was served with the summons. Serve the plaintiff’s complaint with it and copies on all parties. 14.02–14.08 track Fed. R. Civ. P. 14 on third-party defendants’ claims and defenses, the plaintiff’s claims, motions to strike or sever, further impleader, plaintiff impleader, and protective orders.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"15.01","t":"Amendments","s":"Amend once as of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 21 days after service if no response is allowed and the case is not on the trial calendar. Otherwise by leave (freely given) or written consent. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left or 14 days after service, whichever is longer. Differs from the federal 21-days-after-response window.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"15.02","t":"Amendments to conform to the evidence","s":"Issues tried by consent are treated as pleaded; amendment may be allowed on objection unless prejudice is shown, with a continuance available.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"15.03","t":"Relation back","s":"Amendments arising from the same conduct relate back. A change of party relates back if, within the limitations period, the new party had notice so as not to be prejudiced and knew or should have known that but for a mistake about identity it would have been sued.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"15.04","t":"Supplemental pleadings","s":"On motion and reasonable notice, the court may permit supplemental pleadings about later events.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling","s":"The court may hold pretrial conferences (16.01). On any party’s written request it must enter a scheduling order limiting time to join parties, amend, hear motions, and complete discovery; a schedule may be modified only for good cause (16.02). Subjects include issue simplification, admissions, Rule 702 limits, summary adjudication, discovery, ADR, and time limits (16.03). Final pretrial conference attended by trial counsel (16.04); pretrial orders control, modified only to prevent manifest injustice (16.05); sanctions and mandatory expenses for noncompliance (16.06). See also Gen. R. Prac. 111 (scheduling order within 90 days after filing).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"17","t":"Real party in interest; capacity; guardians ad litem","s":"Actions are prosecuted by the real party in interest, with time allowed for ratification or substitution (17.01). Minors and incompetent persons sue through a representative or a court-appointed guardian ad litem, who must be a Minnesota resident, file a consent and oath, and post any required bond; a minor over 14 may apply; applications must be under oath with listed contents; notice is mandatory where the applicant may be adverse (17.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"Any number of claims may be joined. Claims normally requiring a prior judgment, such as setting aside a fraudulent conveyance, may be joined with the money claim.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Join a person needed for complete relief or whose interest may be impaired or who exposes a party to inconsistent obligations (19.01). If joinder is not feasible, the court weighs prejudice, shaping of relief, adequacy, and the plaintiff’s alternative remedy (19.02). Pleadings must name unjoined Rule 19 persons and reasons (19.03). Subject to Rule 23 (19.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined for claims arising from the same transaction or series with a common question; the court may order separate trials or other protective orders.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"A stakeholder exposed to multiple liability may interplead claimants, including by cross-claim or counterclaim, and a defendant who deposits the fund may be discharged.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"23.01–23.04","t":"Class actions: prerequisites, types, certification, conduct","s":"Numerosity, commonality, typicality, and adequacy, plus one of three types (risk of inconsistent adjudications, injunctive or declaratory relief, or predominance and superiority). Certify at an early practicable time, define the class, and appoint class counsel. Best practicable notice with opt-out for 23.02(c) classes; issue classes and subclasses; orders on conduct of the action.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"23.05","t":"Class settlement; residual funds","s":"Settlement, voluntary dismissal, or compromise needs court approval after notice and a fairness hearing, disclosure of side agreements, possible second opt-out, and objections withdrawn only with approval. Minnesota-specific: before distributing residual funds, the court must give notice to potential recipients and to the State Board of Civil Legal Aid and set a deadline for statements (amended July 1, 2025).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"23.06","t":"Class-certification appeals","s":"The Court of Appeals may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification; apply within the Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 105 time. No automatic stay.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"23.07–23.10","t":"Class counsel, fees, derivative and association actions","s":"Appointment criteria for class counsel and interim counsel (23.07); attorney fee motions with notice, objections, and findings (23.08); derivative actions require contemporaneous ownership, a particularized demand allegation, adequacy, and court approval of dismissal or compromise (23.09); actions by or against unincorporated associations (23.10).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right for an impaired, inadequately represented interest (24.01) or permissive for a common question (24.02). Minnesota procedure: serve and file a notice of intervention with a pleading; if no party objects within 30 days after service, intervention is accomplished. If someone objects, the intervenor must serve a motion within 30 days after service of the objection (24.03). With all parties’ written consent, intervene without notice.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On death, the court may substitute proper parties on motion served on parties under Rule 5 and on nonparties under Rule 4; no fixed time limit appears in the rule (25.01). Incompetency (25.02), transfer of interest (25.03), and public officers who leave office (25.04, requiring a showing of substantial need).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.01","t":"Required disclosures","s":"Initial disclosures (witnesses, documents, damages computation, insurance) are due within 60 days after the original answer due date unless otherwise set; parties joined later have 30 days. Many proceedings are exempt (family, Torrens, conciliation appeals, harassment, name changes, defaults, and others). Expert disclosures with signed reports for retained experts, absent order, at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days). Pretrial disclosures at least 30 days before trial; objections within 14 days or waived except under Evidence Rules 402 and 403.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.02","t":"Scope and limits of discovery","s":"Nonprivileged matter relevant to any claim or defense and proportional to the needs of the case. Limits for inaccessible ESI, cumulative or duplicative discovery. Insurance coverage discoverable. Work product and statement rules. Expert discovery by interrogatory, with further discovery on motion; consulting experts only under Rule 35.02 or exceptional circumstances. Privilege logs and clawback of inadvertently produced material.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.03","t":"Protective orders","s":"On motion and good cause, the court may limit or condition discovery, including trade secret protection and sealed filings; expenses under Rule 37.01(d).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.04","t":"Timing and sequence of discovery","s":"No discovery from any source until the parties confer and prepare the Rule 26.06 discovery plan, except in exempt proceedings or by stipulation or order. Rule 34 requests may be delivered more than 21 days after service but are deemed served at the conference. Methods may be used in any sequence. Expedited-track rules may modify timing.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.05","t":"Supplementation","s":"Supplement disclosures and responses in a timely manner when materially incomplete or incorrect; expert supplementation by the pretrial-disclosure date.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.06","t":"Discovery conference and plan","s":"Confer as soon as practicable and within 30 days from the initial answer due date; file a written discovery-plan report within 14 days after the conference or when the action is filed, whichever is later. The plan addresses disclosures, subjects and phasing, ESI, privilege, limits, and orders. The court must hold a discovery conference on a qualifying motion; objections to the motion within 14 days.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"26.07","t":"Signing disclosures and discovery","s":"Every disclosure, request, response, and objection must be signed with address, e-mail, and phone; signing certifies completeness and propriety. Improper certification requires sanctions.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition in the county of an expected adverse party’s residence, with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing, may obtain an order to perpetuate testimony (27.01). The district court may allow depositions pending appeal (27.02). Independent actions remain available (27.03).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions are taken","s":"Officers authorized to administer oaths or court appointees in the U.S. (28.01); treaties, letters of request, notice, or commission abroad (28.02); no officer who is a relative, employee, attorney, or financially interested, or who has a contract that tends to affect impartiality (28.03).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"29","t":"Discovery stipulations","s":"Parties may stipulate to deposition procedures and modify discovery limits, but extensions of Rules 33, 34, or 36 response times that would interfere with discovery, motion, or trial dates need court approval.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.01","t":"When depositions may be taken","s":"After service of the summons, any party may depose any person. The plaintiff needs leave to depose within 30 days after service on any defendant, unless a defendant has sought discovery. Note Rule 26.04(a)’s bar on discovery before the discovery conference.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.02","t":"Notice; recording; remote depositions; organizations","s":"Reasonable written notice stating the deponent, place, and whether in person or by remote technology; objections to the in-person or remote choice require a meet-and-confer, and the objector must show good cause. Audio, video, or stenographic recording at the noticing party’s cost; additional recording methods allowed. Organizational depositions require a duty to confer on topics. A remote deposition is taken where the deponent answers.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.03","t":"Examination; objections noted","s":"Examination proceeds as at trial except Evidence Rules 103 and 615; objections are noted and testimony is taken subject to them; written questions may be sent through the noticing party.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.04","t":"Objections; 7-hour limit; termination","s":"Objections must be concise and non-argumentative; instruct not to answer only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate. One day of seven hours unless extended. Sanctions for impeding. Motion to terminate or limit for bad faith or harassment.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.05","t":"Review by witness","s":"If requested before the deposition ends, the deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign a statement of changes.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"30.06–30.07","t":"Certification, custody, and failure to attend","s":"The officer certifies and sends the deposition to the attorney who arranged it, who must keep it safe (it is not filed); exhibits may be annexed. Expenses may be awarded if the noticing party fails to attend or fails to subpoena a nonparty who then does not appear.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions on written questions","s":"Leave is needed if the witness is in prison or already deposed without stipulation. Cross questions within 14 days after service; redirect within 7 days; recross within 7 days.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions","s":"Usable as admissible under the Evidence Rules against parties present or noticed: for impeachment; a party’s or its designee’s deposition by an adversary for any purpose; any witness’s if dead, more than 100 miles away or out of state, unable to attend, unsubpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances; and with rule-of-completeness rights (32.01). Objections at trial (32.02); jury trials use nonstenographic form on request (32.03); waiver of errors (32.04, including written-question objections within 7 days after the last questions); video depositions usable like stenographic ones (32.05).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories","s":"No more than 50 interrogatories, counting each subpart, without leave on motion and good cause. Answer or object within 30 days after service, but a defendant has at least 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Objections must be specific; answers are signed under oath or penalty of perjury with the prescribed declaration, date, county, and state, and must restate each interrogatory. Contention interrogatories may be deferred. Business-records option. The federal limit is 25.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"34","t":"Production and entry on land","s":"Requests for documents, ESI, things, or entry may be served with or after the summons and complaint (but see Rule 26.04(b) on early requests). Respond within 30 days after service (or deemed service). State whether materials are withheld on each objection; produce as kept or by category; ESI in the specified, ordinary, or reasonably usable form. Nonparties by Rule 45 subpoena.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical, mental, and blood examinations","s":"On motion and good cause where condition is in controversy (35.01); exchange of reports and waiver of privilege by requesting one (35.02). Placing one’s condition in controversy waives medical privilege (35.03). Minnesota-specific: within 14 days of a written request, the waiving party must provide medical reports and signed authorizations for records; treating or examining experts may be deposed only by court order for good cause (35.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but a defendant need not respond before 45 days after service of the summons and complaint. Lack of knowledge requires reasonable inquiry. Motions to test sufficiency. Admissions are conclusive unless withdrawn on motion, and only for the pending action.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.01","t":"Motions to compel","s":"Motions to a party go to the court where the action is pending; to a nonparty, to the court in the county where discovery is taken. A good-faith conference certification is required. Evasive answers are treated as failures. Expenses to the winner unless substantially justified.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.02","t":"Failure to comply with an order","s":"Contempt by the deposition-county court; issue, preclusion, striking, stay, dismissal, default, and contempt by the trial court, plus mandatory expenses unless substantially justified.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.03","t":"Failure to disclose, supplement, or admit","s":"Undisclosed information or witnesses are excluded unless substantially justified or harmless, with expenses, jury instruction, or other sanctions. Costs of proving matters wrongly not admitted, subject to listed exceptions.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.04","t":"Failure to attend own deposition or respond","s":"Sanctions and mandatory expenses; the failure is not excused because the discovery was objectionable unless a protective order was sought.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.05","t":"Lost ESI","s":"If ESI that should have been preserved is lost through unreasonable steps and cannot be replaced: on prejudice, cures no greater than necessary; only on intent to deprive, adverse presumption or instruction, dismissal, or default. Tracks Fed. R. Civ. P. 37(e).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"37.06","t":"Discovery-plan failure","s":"Expenses for failing to participate in good faith in the Rule 26.06 plan.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Issues of fact in actions for money only or specific property are tried to a jury unless waived or referred (38.01). In contract actions, and with permission in others, waiver is by failing to appear, written consent filed, or oral consent in open court. Failing to file a jury demand or pay a jury fee is not a waiver (38.02). Unlike Fed. R. Civ. P. 38, no written demand is required to preserve the right.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by court; advisory jury; preliminary instructions; openings","s":"Non-jury issues are tried by the court (39.01); advisory or consensual juries (39.02); preliminary instructions after the jury is sworn (39.03); plaintiff opens first and others may reserve (39.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"Judges set trial calendars by order or rule.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"41.01","t":"Voluntary dismissal","s":"Without order by notice before service of an answer or summary judgment motion, or by stipulation signed by all parties who have appeared; without prejudice unless stated, but a second notice dismissal of the same claim is an adjudication on the merits. Otherwise by court order; a pending counterclaim must be able to remain.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"41.02","t":"Involuntary dismissal","s":"For failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or orders; motion for dismissal after plaintiff rests in a bench trial (with findings). Unless the order says otherwise, dismissals other than for lack of jurisdiction, forum non conveniens, or failure to join an indispensable party operate on the merits.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"41.03–41.04","t":"Counterclaims; costs of prior action","s":"Dismissal rules apply to counterclaims, cross-claims, and third-party claims. A plaintiff who refiles may be ordered to pay costs of the dismissed action, with a stay until paid.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Consolidation or joint hearings for common questions (42.01); separate trials for convenience, prejudice, or economy (42.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony","s":"Testimony is oral in open court unless otherwise provided (43.01); affirmation in lieu of oath (43.04); motions may be heard on affidavits or oral testimony (43.05); court-appointed interpreters paid from public funds (43.07). Rules 43.02, 43.03, and 43.06 are abrogated.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official records","s":"Domestic records by official publication or attested copy with custody certificate; foreign records by attested copy with final certification (excusable for good cause) or treaty method; certificate of no record; other lawful methods. Rule 44.04 is abrogated.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.01","t":"Subpoena form and issuance","s":"Issued in the name of the court where the action is pending, by the administrator (signed in blank) or by an attorney as officer of the court. Must notify the recipient of the right to compensation. Any use other than to compel trial attendance without prior notice to all parties is improper and sanctionable.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.02","t":"Service of subpoena","s":"Any nonparty 18 or older; personal or abode delivery and tender of one day’s fees and mileage (not required for the State). Document subpoenas require notice to all parties at least 7 days before production. Statewide service. Reasonable compensation arrangements before compliance.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.03","t":"Protecting subpoenaed persons","s":"Duty to avoid undue burden. Written objections to production within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner) stop production absent an order. The court must quash if the subpoena requires a nonparty to travel outside the county where the person resides, works, or does business (except statewide for trial), seeks privileged matter, or imposes undue burden. Nonparty professional witnesses are entitled to reasonable compensation.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.04","t":"Duties in responding","s":"Produce as kept or by category; ESI form rules; inaccessible ESI showing. The issuing party must share produced materials with all parties and give 7 days’ notice of rescheduled production. Privilege logs and clawback.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.05","t":"Contempt","s":"Unexcused failure to obey may be contempt; a subpoena exceeding Rule 45.03(c)(1)(B) territorial limits is an adequate excuse.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"45.06","t":"Interstate depositions and discovery","s":"Minnesota’s version of the Uniform Interstate Depositions and Discovery Act: submit the foreign subpoena to the administrator in the county where discovery is sought, or a Minnesota attorney may issue it. Submitting is not an appearance but subjects the filer to Minnesota jurisdiction and the Rules of Professional Conduct. Minnesota law governs compliance; motions go to that county’s district court.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are not needed; making the objection and grounds known suffices.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court or the parties may conduct voir dire, with supplementation allowed; supplemental questionnaires are not public unless admitted (47.01). The jury may separate overnight during deliberations (47.03). Jurors may be excused for good cause (47.04). Rule 47.02 (alternates) is abrogated.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; unanimity","s":"Six to twelve jurors, all participating in the verdict unless excused. Unless law or stipulation provides otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous and may not be taken from fewer than six jurors.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"Special verdicts; omitted issues are waived unless a jury submission is demanded before retirement. Minnesota-specific: in comparative-fault cases under Minn. Stat. ch. 604, the court informs the jury of the effect of its fault answers and lets counsel comment, unless that would mislead (49.01(b)). General verdicts with interrogatories (49.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; conditional new-trial rulings","s":"Move during trial before submission (50.01). Make or renew the motion after trial within the Rule 59 new-trial time; a pre-verdict motion is not a prerequisite (50.02). Conditional rulings on any new-trial motion (50.03). On denial, the winner may assert new-trial grounds on appeal (50.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"51","t":"Jury instructions","s":"Requests at the close of evidence or earlier as directed (51.01); the court discloses proposed instructions before argument and allows objections on the record (51.02); objections must be distinct and timely (51.03); unpreserved plain error affecting substantial rights may be considered (51.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; amendment","s":"Special findings and conclusions in bench trials and on interlocutory injunctions; clearly erroneous review; no findings needed on Rule 12 or 56 motions except as provided (52.01). A motion to amend findings must be served and heard within the Rule 59.03 new-trial times; sufficiency of evidence may be raised on appeal without such a motion (52.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Appointment only by consent, for exceptional conditions or accountings in bench matters, or for matters a judge cannot timely address; disqualification, notice, order contents (including e-filing use), authority, orders, and reports. Object to a master’s order or report within 21 days after service; de novo review of fact and law objections unless stipulated. Compensation allocation. Statutory referees are covered only if the reference says so.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Judgment defined (54.01). Final judgment on fewer than all claims or parties only on an express no-just-reason-for-delay determination and direction (54.02). Default judgments may not exceed or differ from the demand (54.03). Costs: serve and file a sworn application within 45 days after entry of final judgment; objections within 7 days; taxation by the administrator or judge; appeal of administrator taxation within 7 days, response 7 days (54.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"On affidavit of default: for a sum certain on contract (or taxes), the administrator enters judgment on affidavit of amount due, not exceeding the complaint or a Rule 4 notice of amount. Otherwise apply to the court; a party who has appeared gets 14 days’ notice before the hearing. A bond may be required after publication or out-of-state service, except in title and foreclosure actions. Notes must be filed (55.01). Applies to all claimants (55.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Grant if no genuine issue of material fact and the movant is entitled to judgment; state reasons (56.01). Serve and file under Gen. R. Prac. 115.03 (28 days before hearing) and never less than 14 days before the hearing; unless the court orders otherwise, no motion more than 30 days after the close of all discovery (56.02). Record-cited support, admissibility objections, affidavits on personal knowledge (56.03); Rule 56.04 relief when facts are unavailable; handling of unsupported facts; judgment independent of the motion; partial relief; bad-faith affidavits (56.05–56.08).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Procedure under Minn. Stat. ch. 555 follows these rules; jury rights preserved; another remedy does not bar relief; speedy hearing allowed.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment; stay of entry","s":"The administrator enters judgment forthwith on a general verdict or a money-only order unless the court directs otherwise; the court directs entry on special verdicts or other relief. Entry is not delayed for costs, and the judgment is effective only on entry in the judgment roll (58.01). Entry may be stayed pending post-trial motions and then for up to 30 days; security may be required beyond 30 days (58.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials","s":"Grounds: irregularity, misconduct, accident or surprise, newly discovered evidence, passion or prejudice in damages, preserved errors of law, verdict not justified by evidence or contrary to law (59.01). Heard on the files, exhibits, minutes, and affidavits (59.02). Serve the notice within 30 days after a general verdict or service of notice of filing of the decision; hear within 60 days unless extended for good cause within that period (59.03). Opposing affidavits within 14 days (59.04). Court’s own motion within 14 days (59.05). A stay of entry does not extend the time (59.06). Measured from service of notice of filing, unlike the federal 28 days from entry.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical errors at any time; during appeal with appellate leave (60.01). Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, fraud, void judgment, satisfaction, or any other reason, within a reasonable time and for the first three grounds within one year. Marriage dissolution decrees are excluded. The motion does not affect finality. Independent actions and fraud on the court preserved (60.02).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"62","t":"Stays of enforcement","s":"Discretionary stays pending Rule 50.02, 52.02, 59, or 60 motions (62.01); injunctions pending appeal (62.02); stays on appeal only under Minn. R. Civ. App. P. 107 and 108 (62.03); no bond for the State (62.04); appellate powers preserved (62.05); stays of Rule 54.02 partial judgments (62.06).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability, disqualification, and removal of judge","s":"Another judge may complete post-verdict duties or grant a new trial (63.01). A judge disqualified under the Code of Judicial Conduct may not sit (63.02). Notice to remove: any party may serve and file a peremptory notice within 10 days after notice of the presiding judge, before the trial or hearing starts, but not against a judge who has presided at a motion or other proceeding or who was assigned by the Chief Justice; after one removal as of right, only for cause (63.03). Chief Justice assignment when no qualified district judge (63.04). No federal counterpart to the peremptory notice.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"Provisional seizure remedies are available as provided by state law.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"TROs without notice need affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable harm and counsel’s written statement of notice efforts; the affidavit must be served with the TRO; the temporary injunction hearing is set at the earliest practicable time; the adverse party may move to dissolve (65.01). Temporary injunctions need notice; trial may be consolidated (65.02). Security is required for any TRO or temporary injunction; sureties submit to jurisdiction (65.03). Orders must state reasons and describe acts specifically; binding on parties and those in active concert with actual notice (65.04). No fixed TRO expiration period appears in the rule.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"Receivership actions are dismissed only by order; foreign receivers may sue but are subordinate to local creditors; administration follows Minn. Stat. ch. 576.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"Deposit with leave in an action (67.01); a stakeholder without a pending action may petition to deposit disputed property and be discharged (67.02); the court may order deposit of admitted trust property, enforceable by contempt (67.03); funds deposited in the administrator’s bank account (67.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment or settlement","s":"Any party may serve, more than 14 days before trial, a written offer of judgment or settlement that expressly refers to Rule 68. Offers are ‘damages-only’ (interest, costs, and applicable fees added) unless expressly ‘total-obligation.’ Acceptance within 14 days; the offer is irrevocable during that time and then deemed withdrawn; a later offer supersedes. If a plaintiff rejects a defendant’s offer and does worse, the plaintiff pays the defendant’s post-offer costs and loses its own; if a defendant rejects a plaintiff’s offer and does worse, the defendant pays an amount equal to the plaintiff’s post-offer costs. Attorney fees are unaffected. The court may reduce for undue hardship. Unlike the federal rule, plaintiffs may make offers.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution","s":"Money judgments are enforced by writ of execution under Minn. Stat. ch. 550; judgment creditors may take discovery from any person.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform, the court may appoint another to act, issue a writ of attachment, hold the party in contempt, or vest title by judgment; writs of possession issue on application.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"71","t":"Process for or against nonparties","s":"Nonparties may enforce orders in their favor and are subject to process to enforce orders against them.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"72–76","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved for future use.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"77","t":"District courts and court administrators","s":"Courts are always open for filing (77.01); trials in open court; chambers proceedings anywhere, but no non-ex parte hearing outside the district without consent (77.02); administrator acts of course, subject to court review (77.03). The administrator must notify parties on filing of an order or entry of judgment by mail, e-mail, or the e-filing system, but that notice does not limit appeal times (77.04).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"78–79","t":"Reserved","s":"Reserved for future use.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"80","t":"Transcript as evidence","s":"Reported prior testimony admissible at a later trial may be proved by a certified transcript; rebuttable.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability","s":"Statutory proceedings listed in Appendix A keep their procedures to the extent inconsistent with the rules; statutes in Appendix B and other conflicting procedural statutes are superseded (81.01). Statutes on appeals to district court are not superseded (81.02). Statutes requiring acts ‘as provided by law’ follow these rules (81.03).","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue","s":"The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"83","t":"District court rules","s":"Courts may recommend local rules not in conflict with these rules or the General Rules of Practice, effective only as ordered by the Supreme Court.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"84–85","t":"Forms; title","s":"The appendix forms illustrate sufficient pleading. Cited as the Rules of Civil Procedure.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"Original rules effective January 1, 1952. Unless the Court specifies otherwise, amendments take effect January 1 or July 1 in the year of or after adoption and apply to pending cases unless infeasible or unjust.","u":"https://www.revisor.mn.gov/court_rules/cp/pdf/"}]});
